{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WeDGUWEvEnevh8u6jrXA2w","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tCreated-Local: 2026-10-13 +07\n2\t# Slice 7b: holder export, recovery v2, and driver lower\n3\t\n4\tImplemented and verified in `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7b`, branch `srvm-phase7b`. No commit. JVM only; every test process ran in the foreground, sequentially, including the mutation runs. No Node, Dart, remote host, network fetch, or escalation was needed.\n5\t\n6\t## Result\n7\t\n8\t7b's acceptance passes. The holder exports row packs under the composition's source index, freezes recovery format 2 with one pack and holder-local metadata, and rehydrates through fenced row lowering without program IO or child initialization. Exact counters, cursor aliases/distinct cells, all parked records, authentic receiver-owned values, operation IDs and issue stamps survive recovery. The recovered record reproduces the published pack byte for byte and cannot abort into execution. Q3's link/parent and manifest/image checks refuse before attachment. The driver's validation, grant lower and restart paths use rows; `:export-version` is removed from the driver/composition.\n9\t\n10\tThe existing partial edit was reviewed first with `git diff`, then by production-file review and tests. Its row export/driver/recovery implementation was retained. I removed its copied legacy holder export implementation from `lift_support`, moved the five preparation tests to the row holder suite, and added a two-record parked recovery test. The legacy codec is exercised directly through its existing `handoff/export-task`; no compatibility shim or old recovery reader was added.\n11\t\n12\tThis is integration-branch readiness for **7b**, not readiness to land the entire phase on master. The deferred red suites are listed below.\n13\t\n14\t## Files and line counts\n15\t\n16\tCounts are final file lines and added/deleted lines versus this worktree's HEAD, including the retained partial edit.\n17\t\n18\t| File | Final lines | Added | Deleted |\n19\t|---|---:|---:|---:|\n20\t| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/compose.cljc` | 1009 | 11 | 6 |\n21\t| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc` | 2943 | 82 | 56 |\n22\t| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export.cljc` | 628 | 302 | 242 |\n23\t| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` | 2262 | 66 | 23 |\n24\t| `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` | 946 | 306 | 277 |\n25\t| `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc` | 1939 | 11 | 2 |\n26\t| `test/yin/vm/ucf/handoff_v2_holder_test.cljc` | 217 | 5 | 156 |\n27\t| `test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver_test.cljc` | 4255 | 45 | 45 |\n28\t| `test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export_test.cljc` | 974 | 625 | 563 |\n29\t| `test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/reader_test.cljc` | 1445 | 1 | 1 |\n30\t| `test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/writer_test.cljc` | 1127 | 6 | 5 |\n31\t| `test/yin/vm/ucf/lift_support.cljc` | 454 | 3 | 21 |\n32\t| `test/yin/vm/ucf/lift_v1_test.cljc` | 471 | 2 | 138 |\n33\t| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc` | 315 | 40 | 0 |\n34\t| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_support.cljc` | 297 | 64 | 5 |\n35\t\n36\t`holder/evidence_test.cljc`, `rows_test.cljc`, and `layout_test.cljc` needed no edit. The harness change only supplies complete linker-style manifest/derivation data for Q3's install fixtures; its existing assertions and root pins were not edited. The rows checkpoint resource was regenerated for the extended install responses and is verified against its renderer. Production `handoff.cljc` and the old inspector/codec implementations remain untouched.\n37\t\n38\t## Implementation details that matter to review\n39\t\n40\t- `holder.export/prepare` retains served answers by `[task-path resource-id]`, resolves handle aliases from the retained record, and passes `:header`, `:source-index` and `:serve-keyed` to `rows/lift-task`. `encode` reads only those answers; it never serves externally.\n41\t- Recovery format 2 contains exactly the tag, version, pack address, structural root, base64 pack, local header, and `:yin.k/state` (gate, origins, issued, ordered issues, child metadata). There is no second snapshot body, duplicate descriptor set, current lease, epoch or holder in the pack. The origin's predecessor lease remains as the specified lineage evidence.\n42\t- Rehydration validates the recovery address, pack address/grammar, root, header/custody agreement and state/tree shape before attachment. Fenced lower preserves the exact carried id counter, records resource-key/cursor-cell associations, detaches waits and every parked record, and gates every task exporting. Child construction disables `:make-stream` during administrative spawning, restoring the composition's constructor afterward.\n43\t- Q3 checks the response's `:yin.link/id` against the install parent and checks its image through the manifest-named semantic derivation record: record address, tree input and image output. It adds no fetcher, resolver or initialization path.\n44\t- The driver still mints and journals occurrences before prepare, stores recovery before the pack, and preserves the existing fence/offer/report/release brackets and crash-cut names. The source index is a required composition seam, supplied as `(fn [] {A #{tree-address}})`.\n45\t- 7a D4 is preserved: lift serves during its walk before deciding exhaustion/protection refusals. The record retains those answers, and the retry test proves no successful serve is repeated.\n46\t\n47\t## Verification\n48\t\n49\tAll changed Clojure files were linted with `clj -M:kondo --lint <changed files>`: **0 errors, 0 warnings**. `git diff --check` is clean.\n50\t\n51\tUnique successful namespace coverage (excluding duplicate focused/post-mutation runs): **514 quick tests / 8,941 assertions**, plus **5 slow tests / 3,113 assertions**, all with zero failures and errors. Each touched namespace was run using `clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns>` and `-i :slow`; the two support namespaces correctly select zero tests. All five holder namespaces and all eight authority namespaces were included. The rows checkpoint suite was also run in both tiers.\n52\t\n53\t| Namespace | Quick tests / assertions | Slow tests / assertions |\n54\t|---|---:|---:|\n55\t| `yin.vm.semantic-register.layout-test` | 12 / 2396 | 0 / 0 |\n56\t| `yin.vm.semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test` | 23 / 1213 | 3 / 2716 |\n57\t| `yin.vm.store-write-audit-test` | 3 / 129 | 0 / 0 |\n58\t| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.admission-test` | 23 / 240 | 0 / 0 |\n59\t| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.completion-fold-test` | 8 / 50 | 0 / 0 |\n60\t| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.completion-test` | 25 / 303 | 0 / 0 |\n61\t| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.front-test` | 30 / 217 | 0 / 0 |\n62\t| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.grant-test` | 16 / 107 | 0 / 0 |\n63\t| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.inherited-test` | 18 / 112 | 0 / 0 |\n64\t| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.input-test` | 20 / 125 | 0 / 0 |\n65\t| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.reclaim-test` | 21 / 108 | 0 / 0 |\n66\t| `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint-test` | 15 / 247 | 0 / 0 |\n67\t| `yin.vm.ucf.handoff-v2-holder-test` | 5 / 338 | 0 / 0 |\n68\t| `yin.vm.ucf.holder.driver-test` | 116 / 1524 | 1 / 389 |\n69\t| `yin.vm.ucf.holder.evidence-test` | 9 / 38 | 0 / 0 |\n70\t| `yin.vm.ucf.holder.export-test` | 36 / 325 | 0 / 0 |\n71\t| `yin.vm.ucf.holder.reader-test` | 24 / 247 | 0 / 0 |\n72\t| `yin.vm.ucf.holder.writer-test` | 25 / 202 | 0 / 0 |\n73\t| `yin.vm.ucf.ledger-fixture-test` | 6 / 53 | 0 / 0 |\n74\t| `yin.vm.ucf.lift-support` | 0 / 0 | 0 / 0 |\n75\t| `yin.vm.ucf.lift-v1-test` | 21 / 208 | 0 / 0 |\n76\t| `yin.vm.ucf.rows-checkpoint-test` | 17 / 179 | 0 / 0 |\n77\t| `yin.vm.ucf.rows-custody-test` | 12 / 127 | 0 / 0 |\n78\t| `yin.vm.ucf.rows-support` | 0 / 0 | 0 / 0 |\n79\t| `yin.vm.ucf.rows-test` | 29 / 453 | 1 / 8 |\n80\t\n81\tThe driver has **117 deftests total** (116 quick, one slow), as required. Rows, layout, and the safepoint harness retain their assertions; no existing pinned root was changed. The store-write audit, old checkpoint suite, old direct v1 lift tests and remaining v2 holder tests also pass. After restoring every mutant, the focused export/custody/checkpoint gate passed again: **65 tests / 631 assertions**.\n82\t\n83\tLogs and machine-readable results: `target/7b-verification/`, `results.json`, `final-checks.json`, `final-lint.log`, and `mutants/results.json`. These are local ignored verification artifacts.\n84\t\n85\t### Deferred red suites, observed unchanged\n86\t\n87\tThese quick-tier probes were run to identify the integration boundary; they are excluded from the green acceptance totals. No fixes were made in these suites.\n88\t\n89\t| Namespace | Tests / assertions | Failures / errors | Follow-up |\n90\t|---|---:|---:|---|\n91\t| `yin.vm.ucf.compose-test` | 14 / 23 | 3 / 20 | 7c: row machines and source-index wiring |\n92\t| `yin.vm.ucf.compose-rows-test` | 39 / 39 | 0 / 39 | 7c: row machines, source-index and recovery-v2 assertions |\n93\t| `yin.vm.ucf.fencing-rows-test` | 4 / 61 | 29 / 2 | 7c: row lift/lower and rows authority inspector |\n94\t| `yin.vm.ucf.stage-e-rows-test` | 5 / 5 | 0 / 5 | 7d: port the world and its composition seams |\n95\t| `yin.vm.ucf.handoff-v2-authority-test` | 3 / 68 | 8 / 0 | Known 7a D1: old v2 bodies are refused by the rows-only authority |\n96\t\n97\tThe Stage E process suite was not run: it requires the JVM/Node peer matrix, prohibited by this brief's JVM-only/no-Node instruction. Deferred compose/Stage E slow tiers belong to 7c/7d and were not run here. `yin.repl` was not touched, per owner Q2.\n98\t\n99\t## Mutations\n100\t\n101\tEach mutant ran in a fresh foreground JVM against `holder.export-test` or `rows-custody-test`. Sources were copied to backups first; each mutant's diff and test log were saved. After each run, restoration was checked byte for byte, with `git diff --quiet --no-index <backup> <source>`, and by comparing the entire binary worktree diff with its pre-mutation diff. Plain `git diff --quiet` against HEAD cannot be clean in this intentionally uncommitted worktree; no-index verifies restoration without erasing the intended edit. All nine behavior-changing mutants were killed; none survived.\n102\t\n103\t| Mutant | Failures / errors | Representative catching test |\n104\t|---|---:|---|\n105\t| `snapshot-body` | 1 / 0 | `the-recovery-object-is-the-pack-and-holder-local-state-test` |\n106\t| `program-execution` | 6 / 0 | `complete-export-recovery-is-fenced-and-reproducible-test` |\n107\t| `counter-off-by-one` | 64 / 4 | `the-id-counter-is-carried-exactly-test` |\n108\t| `parked-record-dropped` | 14 / 0 | `recovery-retains-every-parked-record-test` |\n109\t| `restart-abort-accepted` | 1 / 0 | `a-restarted-abort-is-refused-test` |\n110\t| `install-link-check-removed` | 4 / 0 | `the-install-response-is-verified-before-any-attachment` |\n111\t| `install-image-check-removed` | 10 / 0 | `the-install-response-is-verified-before-any-attachment` |\n112\t| `source-index-omitted` | 81 / 10 | `the-lift-takes-trees-from-the-composition-source-index-test` |\n113\t| `attach-before-custody` | 30 / 0 | `every-grant-check-refuses-before-any-attachment` |\n114\t\n115\tThe program-execution mutant reinstated recovery waits, removed the exporting gate and called `vm/run` during rehydration; the zero-IO assertions caught the resulting operations. The attachment-order mutant attached first and then ran custody; 30 zero-attachment assertions failed. Snapshot duplication was detected by the recovery object's exact-key assertion. The Q3 checks were removed separately, not together.\n116\t\n117\t## Deviations\n118\t\n119\t1. **Five old-suite preparation tests moved to `holder.export-test`.** They exercised the retired holder wrapper, not merely the old codec. The initial partial edit copied that wrapper into test support. I removed the copy and moved the tests onto real semantic-register machines; direct v0/v1 coverage remains on the old codec. `lift_support/lift` now calls the existing codec directly, and the child hold/path assertion calls the kernel-agnostic `export/enter`.\n120\t2. **Q3 required fixture completion beyond holder files.** `rows_support` and the harness's module-response builder now carry the manifest tree, derivation address and record. The row checkpoint resource therefore changes for install-containing cases. Existing row/layout root pins remain unchanged.\n121\t3. **No duplicate recovery descriptor set.** The pack already carries every stream descriptor. Rehydration derives the internal retained served table from those descriptors and administrative attachments. This follows the owner's “only holder-local engine state beside the pack” instruction and avoids persisting the same facts twice.\n122\t4. **Header validation belongs to `rehydrate-fenced`.** It compares the local recovery header with the inspected pack custody and validates `:yin.k/state` against the task tree before calling `rows/lower-task {:fenced true}`. Raw row lowering does not take a recovery envelope or implement an independent recovery-header reader.\n123\t5. **An additional all-parked-record test** covers an inactive record alongside the selected explicit park; both must survive with no IO and an identical re-encoded pack.\n124\t\n125\t## Design defects / discrepancies (separate list)\n126\t\n127\t- **D1, inherited from 7a:** the rows-only authority rejects old bodies. Compose/fencing/Stage E still need their planned ports, and the old v2 authority test is not repaired by those ports. The latter needs an explicit owner disposition before final phase acceptance; no version dispatch was introduced.\n128\t- **D2, inherited from 7a:** the plan's different-root/equal-baseline variant conflicts with the actual baseline, which includes the root. Driver equal variants use a different row order with the same root; different roots remain conflicts. Recovery must reproduce canonical emitted bytes, so a merely reordered pack is valid carrier syntax but inconsistent recovery. The existing semantics were preserved and tested.\n129\t- **D3, scope discrepancy:** the plan describes `lift_v1_test` as direct old-codec coverage to keep untouched, but five tests invoked the replaced holder preparation path. Keeping them unchanged would require the forbidden legacy wrapper/shim. Moving those five laws onto rows preserves their subjects without keeping the retired implementation alive.\n130\t- **D4, recovery shape discrepancy:** plan §3.2's sketch includes a separate descriptor set; the owner brief says the pack plus only holder-local state. The implemented format omits the redundant set. Document this final shape in 7e.\n131\t- **D5, known 7a lift ordering fact:** streams may already be served when a custody/protection lift refusal is returned. Accounting and retries preserve that fact; no zero-serve law was invented.\n132\t\n133\tNo additional unresolved production defect was found in the reviewed 7b implementation. Cross-host execution remains unverified here by instruction, not implied by the JVM results.\n134\t\n135\t## What 7c needs\n136\t\n137\t- Supply `:source-index` to `compose/open!` and driver configs as a function returning the composition/linker's current `{A #{tree-address}}`. Do not add a fallback resolver or synthetic index in production.\n138\t- Build source and receiver machines on semantic-register, and use rows for fork/exclusive bodies. The holder answers pack bytes and their BLAKE3 **pack address**; the structural root is distinct and stays in the baseline.\n139\t- Rebase content-store/restart assertions on recovery format 2: `:yin.k/pack-bytes`, `:yin.k/pack-address`, `:yin.k/root`, header and local state; no snapshot or duplicate descriptor set. Keep recovery-first/pack-second puts and the existing journal cut names.\n140\t- Pass grant/protection/exclusive options to row lower. Preserve the no-attachment-before-custody law. Q3 install fixtures must carry a parent-correlated link ID and the manifest-named semantic derivation/image chain.\n141\t- Use same-root/reordered-row packs for equal variants; use different-root packs only as conflicts. Preserve the accepted REPL gap until phase 8.\n142\t- Run the deferred compose/fencing suites and their slow tiers, then hand the world/source-index/recovery changes to 7d for the Stage E matrix. No files in those suites or in `yin.repl` were edited here.\n143\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"72b4f199-a30e-42b5-b648-90d6dc2ab934","uuid":"3bc03618-c450-43ab-9eb9-607ff98a17d8","timestamp":"2026-10-13T13:58:12.859Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7b/collab/1792450000000-engineer-7b-holder-export.report.md","content":"Created-Local: 2026-10-13 +07\n# Slice 7b: holder export, recovery v2, and driver lower\n\nImplemented and verified in `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7b`, branch `srvm-phase7b`. No commit. JVM only; every test process ran in the foreground, sequentially, including the mutation runs. No Node, Dart, remote host, network fetch, or escalation was needed.\n\n## Result\n\n7b's acceptance passes. The holder exports row packs under the composition's source index, freezes recovery format 2 with one pack and holder-local metadata, and rehydrates through fenced row lowering without program IO or child initialization. Exact counters, cursor aliases/distinct cells, all parked records, authentic receiver-owned values, operation IDs and issue stamps survive recovery. The recovered record reproduces the published pack byte for byte and cannot abort into execution. Q3's link/parent and manifest/image checks refuse before attachment. The driver's validation, grant lower and restart paths use rows; `:export-version` is removed from the driver/composition.\n\nThe existing partial edit was reviewed first with `git diff`, then by production-file review and tests. Its row export/driver/recovery implementation was retained. I removed its copied legacy holder export implementation from `lift_support`, moved the five preparation tests to the row holder suite, and added a two-record parked recovery test. The legacy codec is exercised directly through its existing `handoff/export-task`; no compatibility shim or old recovery reader was added.\n\nThis is integration-branch readiness for **7b**, not readiness to land the entire phase on master. The deferred red suites are listed below.\n\n## Files and line counts\n\nCounts are final file lines and added/deleted lines versus this worktree's HEAD, including the retained partial edit.\n\n| File | Final lines | Added | Deleted |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/compose.cljc` | 1009 | 11 | 6 |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver.cljc` | 2943 | 82 | 56 |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export.cljc` | 628 | 302 | 242 |\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` | 2262 | 66 | 23 |\n| `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` | 946 | 306 | 277 |\n| `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc` | 1939 | 11 | 2 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/handoff_v2_holder_test.cljc` | 217 | 5 | 156 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/driver_test.cljc` | 4255 | 45 | 45 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/export_test.cljc` | 974 | 625 | 563 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/reader_test.cljc` | 1445 | 1 | 1 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/holder/writer_test.cljc` | 1127 | 6 | 5 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/lift_support.cljc` | 454 | 3 | 21 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/lift_v1_test.cljc` | 471 | 2 | 138 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc` | 315 | 40 | 0 |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_support.cljc` | 297 | 64 | 5 |\n\n`holder/evidence_test.cljc`, `rows_test.cljc`, and `layout_test.cljc` needed no edit. The harness change only supplies complete linker-style manifest/derivation data for Q3's install fixtures; its existing assertions and root pins were not edited. The rows checkpoint resource was regenerated for the extended install responses and is verified against its renderer. Production `handoff.cljc` and the old inspector/codec implementations remain untouched.\n\n## Implementation details that matter to review\n\n- `holder.export/prepare` retains served answers by `[task-path resource-id]`, resolves handle aliases from the retained record, and passes `:header`, `:source-index` and `:serve-keyed` to `rows/lift-task`. `encode` reads only those answers; it never serves externally.\n- Recovery format 2 contains exactly the tag, version, pack address, structural root, base64 pack, local header, and `:yin.k/state` (gate, origins, issued, ordered issues, child metadata). There is no second snapshot body, duplicate descriptor set, current lease, epoch or holder in the pack. The origin's predecessor lease remains as the specified lineage evidence.\n- Rehydration validates the recovery address, pack address/grammar, root, header/custody agreement and state/tree shape before attachment. Fenced lower preserves the exact carried id counter, records resource-key/cursor-cell associations, detaches waits and every parked record, and gates every task exporting. Child construction disables `:make-stream` during administrative spawning, restoring the composition's constructor afterward.\n- Q3 checks the response's `:yin.link/id` against the install parent and checks its image through the manifest-named semantic derivation record: record address, tree input and image output. It adds no fetcher, resolver or initialization path.\n- The driver still mints and journals occurrences before prepare, stores recovery before the pack, and preserves the existing fence/offer/report/release brackets and crash-cut names. The source index is a required composition seam, supplied as `(fn [] {A #{tree-address}})`.\n- 7a D4 is preserved: lift serves during its walk before deciding exhaustion/protection refusals. The record retains those answers, and the retry test proves no successful serve is repeated.\n\n## Verification\n\nAll changed Clojure files were linted with `clj -M:kondo --lint <changed files>`: **0 errors, 0 warnings**. `git diff --check` is clean.\n\nUnique successful namespace coverage (excluding duplicate focused/post-mutation runs): **514 quick tests / 8,941 assertions**, plus **5 slow tests / 3,113 assertions**, all with zero failures and errors. Each touched namespace was run using `clojure -M:test -e :slow -n <ns>` and `-i :slow`; the two support namespaces correctly select zero tests. All five holder namespaces and all eight authority namespaces were included. The rows checkpoint suite was also run in both tiers.\n\n| Namespace | Quick tests / assertions | Slow tests / assertions |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| `yin.vm.semantic-register.layout-test` | 12 / 2396 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.semantic-register.safepoint-harness-test` | 23 / 1213 | 3 / 2716 |\n| `yin.vm.store-write-audit-test` | 3 / 129 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.admission-test` | 23 / 240 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.completion-fold-test` | 8 / 50 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.completion-test` | 25 / 303 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.front-test` | 30 / 217 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.grant-test` | 16 / 107 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.inherited-test` | 18 / 112 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.input-test` | 20 / 125 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.authority.reclaim-test` | 21 / 108 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint-test` | 15 / 247 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.handoff-v2-holder-test` | 5 / 338 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.holder.driver-test` | 116 / 1524 | 1 / 389 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.holder.evidence-test` | 9 / 38 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.holder.export-test` | 36 / 325 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.holder.reader-test` | 24 / 247 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.holder.writer-test` | 25 / 202 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.ledger-fixture-test` | 6 / 53 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.lift-support` | 0 / 0 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.lift-v1-test` | 21 / 208 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.rows-checkpoint-test` | 17 / 179 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.rows-custody-test` | 12 / 127 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.rows-support` | 0 / 0 | 0 / 0 |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.rows-test` | 29 / 453 | 1 / 8 |\n\nThe driver has **117 deftests total** (116 quick, one slow), as required. Rows, layout, and the safepoint harness retain their assertions; no existing pinned root was changed. The store-write audit, old checkpoint suite, old direct v1 lift tests and remaining v2 holder tests also pass. After restoring every mutant, the focused export/custody/checkpoint gate passed again: **65 tests / 631 assertions**.\n\nLogs and machine-readable results: `target/7b-verification/`, `results.json`, `final-checks.json`, `final-lint.log`, and `mutants/results.json`. These are local ignored verification artifacts.\n\n### Deferred red suites, observed unchanged\n\nThese quick-tier probes were run to identify the integration boundary; they are excluded from the green acceptance totals. No fixes were made in these suites.\n\n| Namespace | Tests / assertions | Failures / errors | Follow-up |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| `yin.vm.ucf.compose-test` | 14 / 23 | 3 / 20 | 7c: row machines and source-index wiring |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.compose-rows-test` | 39 / 39 | 0 / 39 | 7c: row machines, source-index and recovery-v2 assertions |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.fencing-rows-test` | 4 / 61 | 29 / 2 | 7c: row lift/lower and rows authority inspector |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.stage-e-rows-test` | 5 / 5 | 0 / 5 | 7d: port the world and its composition seams |\n| `yin.vm.ucf.handoff-v2-authority-test` | 3 / 68 | 8 / 0 | Known 7a D1: old v2 bodies are refused by the rows-only authority |\n\nThe Stage E process suite was not run: it requires the JVM/Node peer matrix, prohibited by this brief's JVM-only/no-Node instruction. Deferred compose/Stage E slow tiers belong to 7c/7d and were not run here. `yin.repl` was not touched, per owner Q2.\n\n## Mutations\n\nEach mutant ran in a fresh foreground JVM against `holder.export-test` or `rows-custody-test`. Sources were copied to backups first; each mutant's diff and test log were saved. After each run, restoration was checked byte for byte, with `git diff --quiet --no-index <backup> <source>`, and by comparing the entire binary worktree diff with its pre-mutation diff. Plain `git diff --quiet` against HEAD cannot be clean in this intentionally uncommitted worktree; no-index verifies restoration without erasing the intended edit. All nine behavior-changing mutants were killed; none survived.\n\n| Mutant | Failures / errors | Representative catching test |\n|---|---:|---|\n| `snapshot-body` | 1 / 0 | `the-recovery-object-is-the-pack-and-holder-local-state-test` |\n| `program-execution` | 6 / 0 | `complete-export-recovery-is-fenced-and-reproducible-test` |\n| `counter-off-by-one` | 64 / 4 | `the-id-counter-is-carried-exactly-test` |\n| `parked-record-dropped` | 14 / 0 | `recovery-retains-every-parked-record-test` |\n| `restart-abort-accepted` | 1 / 0 | `a-restarted-abort-is-refused-test` |\n| `install-link-check-removed` | 4 / 0 | `the-install-response-is-verified-before-any-attachment` |\n| `install-image-check-removed` | 10 / 0 | `the-install-response-is-verified-before-any-attachment` |\n| `source-index-omitted` | 81 / 10 | `the-lift-takes-trees-from-the-composition-source-index-test` |\n| `attach-before-custody` | 30 / 0 | `every-grant-check-refuses-before-any-attachment` |\n\nThe program-execution mutant reinstated recovery waits, removed the exporting gate and called `vm/run` during rehydration; the zero-IO assertions caught the resulting operations. The attachment-order mutant attached first and then ran custody; 30 zero-attachment assertions failed. Snapshot duplication was detected by the recovery object's exact-key assertion. The Q3 checks were removed separately, not together.\n\n## Deviations\n\n1. **Five old-suite preparation tests moved to `holder.export-test`.** They exercised the retired holder wrapper, not merely the old codec. The initial partial edit copied that wrapper into test support. I removed the copy and moved the tests onto real semantic-register machines; direct v0/v1 coverage remains on the old codec. `lift_support/lift` now calls the existing codec directly, and the child hold/path assertion calls the kernel-agnostic `export/enter`.\n2. **Q3 required fixture completion beyond holder files.** `rows_support` and the harness's module-response builder now carry the manifest tree, derivation address and record. The row checkpoint resource therefore changes for install-containing cases. Existing row/layout root pins remain unchanged.\n3. **No duplicate recovery descriptor set.** The pack already carries every stream descriptor. Rehydration derives the internal retained served table from those descriptors and administrative attachments. This follows the owner's “only holder-local engine state beside the pack” instruction and avoids persisting the same facts twice.\n4. **Header validation belongs to `rehydrate-fenced`.** It compares the local recovery header with the inspected pack custody and validates `:yin.k/state` against the task tree before calling `rows/lower-task {:fenced true}`. Raw row lowering does not take a recovery envelope or implement an independent recovery-header reader.\n5. **An additional all-parked-record test** covers an inactive record alongside the selected explicit park; both must survive with no IO and an identical re-encoded pack.\n\n## Design defects / discrepancies (separate list)\n\n- **D1, inherited from 7a:** the rows-only authority rejects old bodies. Compose/fencing/Stage E still need their planned ports, and the old v2 authority test is not repaired by those ports. The latter needs an explicit owner disposition before final phase acceptance; no version dispatch was introduced.\n- **D2, inherited from 7a:** the plan's different-root/equal-baseline variant conflicts with the actual baseline, which includes the root. Driver equal variants use a different row order with the same root; different roots remain conflicts. Recovery must reproduce canonical emitted bytes, so a merely reordered pack is valid carrier syntax but inconsistent recovery. The existing semantics were preserved and tested.\n- **D3, scope discrepancy:** the plan describes `lift_v1_test` as direct old-codec coverage to keep untouched, but five tests invoked the replaced holder preparation path. Keeping them unchanged would require the forbidden legacy wrapper/shim. Moving those five laws onto rows preserves their subjects without keeping the retired implementation alive.\n- **D4, recovery shape discrepancy:** plan §3.2's sketch includes a separate descriptor set; the owner brief says the pack plus only holder-local state. The implemented format omits the redundant set. Document this final shape in 7e.\n- **D5, known 7a lift ordering fact:** streams may already be served when a custody/protection lift refusal is returned. Accounting and retries preserve that fact; no zero-serve law was invented.\n\nNo additional unresolved production defect was found in the reviewed 7b implementation. Cross-host execution remains unverified here by instruction, not implied by the JVM results.\n\n## What 7c needs\n\n- Supply `:source-index` to `compose/open!` and driver configs as a function returning the composition/linker's current `{A #{tree-address}}`. Do not add a fallback resolver or synthetic index in production.\n- Build source and receiver machines on semantic-register, and use rows for fork/exclusive bodies. The holder answers pack bytes and their BLAKE3 **pack address**; the structural root is distinct and stays in the baseline.\n- Rebase content-store/restart assertions on recovery format 2: `:yin.k/pack-bytes`, `:yin.k/pack-address`, `:yin.k/root`, header and local state; no snapshot or duplicate descriptor set. Keep recovery-first/pack-second puts and the existing journal cut names.\n- Pass grant/protection/exclusive options to row lower. Preserve the no-attachment-before-custody law. Q3 install fixtures must carry a parent-correlated link ID and the manifest-named semantic derivation/image chain.\n- Use same-root/reordered-row packs for equal variants; use different-root packs only as conflicts. Preserve the accepted REPL gap until phase 8.\n- Run the deferred compose/fencing suites and their slow tiers, then hand the world/source-index/recovery changes to 7d for the Stage E matrix. No files in those suites or in `yin.repl` were edited here.\n","numLines":143,"startLine":1,"totalLines":143}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01WeDGUWEvEnevh8u6jrXA2w","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BJwzdj2FJCGN8U6J7tgixE","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tCreated-Local: 2026-10-13 +07\n2\t# Slice 7a report: custody on rows, the rows inspector, the authority's fixtures (opus, engineer)\n3\t\n4\tWorktree `srvm-phase7a`, from `23a8caef`. Nothing committed. JVM only, all runs in the foreground (one quick-tier run of every `yin.vm.ucf.*` namespace went past the 10-minute foreground limit, finished in the background and was read from its log).\n5\t\n6\t## Verdict\n7\t\n8\t7a is done as the plan's slice section and §3.1, §3.3 and §3.4 describe it, and its own acceptance holds: the two new suites, the ledger fixture, crash cuts, the eight authority tests and the holder tests that use fixtures all pass. `rows_test` and the safepoint harness pass with **no root id moved**.\n9\t\n10\t**7a cannot land alone (design defect D1 below).** The plan's switch of the authority to the rows inspector makes the authority refuse every version-1/2 body. 141 deftests in six old-codec D/E suites now fail. Those suites are re-based in 7b/7c, and I proved the switch is the only cause. I did not work around it, because a fix would change semantics; the decision belongs to the orchestrator.\n11\t\n12\t## Verification\n13\t\n14\t- Lint: `clojure -M:kondo` needed approval in this session, so I used `clj -M:kondo --lint <files>`. Final run over all 22 changed `.clj[c]` files: 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n15\t- Quick tier, new and touched namespaces plus `rows_test`, the harness, `layout_test`, the old `checkpoint_test` and `lift_v1_test` (20 namespaces): **360 tests, 7188 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors** (`target/7a-final.log`).\n16\t  - `rows_checkpoint_test`: 17 deftests.\n17\t  - `rows_custody_test`: 11 deftests, 107 assertions before the M3 fix added two more.\n18\t- Slow tier (`-i :slow`) on the same namespaces plus `rows_test` and the harness: 4 tests, 2724 assertions, 0 failures.\n19\t- Every `yin.vm.ucf.*` namespace, quick tier (`-r 'yin\\.vm\\.ucf\\..*'`, `target/7a-ucf-quick.log`): 694 tests, 648 failures and 89 errors. They are all in six suites (see D1):\n20\t\n21\t  | Namespace | Failing deftests | Failing assertions |\n22\t  |---|---|---|\n23\t  | `holder.driver-test` | 92 | 294 |\n24\t  | `compose-rows-test` | 35 | 335 |\n25\t  | `compose-test` | 6 | 35 |\n26\t  | `stage-e-rows-test` | 4 (the quick ones) | 35 |\n27\t  | `fencing-rows-test` | 3 | 30 |\n28\t  | `handoff-v2-authority-test` | 1 | 8 |\n29\t\n30\t  No other `yin.vm.ucf` namespace fails.\n31\t- Counterfactual check: I pointed the three authority requires back at `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint` and ran `fencing-rows-test`, `handoff-v2-authority-test` and `compose-test`: 21 tests, 332 assertions, 0 failures. I then restored the files (byte-compare verified).\n32\t- Node and Dart were not run, as instructed. The new code follows the portability rules: no `for`, no private var access, catches with `:cljd` first, start-from-`{}` assoc, no EDN strings. The `version-float` fixture relies on Node keeping a `Float64` carrier and on Dart's `integer?` rejecting a double, both checked in the source (`rows/decode` gates on `code/reg?`), but this is unverified on those hosts.\n33\t\n34\t## Files\n35\t\n36\tCreated:\n37\t\n38\t| File | Lines | What |\n39\t|---|---|---|\n40\t| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows/checkpoint.cljc` | 102 | The rows inspector: `inspect address bytes`, `inspect-pack wire`. Address check, canonical CBOR, `rows/decode`, a `:task` root, then `rows/custody-baseline`; adds `:yin.k/root` |\n41\t| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_support.cljc` | 238 | Semantic-register machine builders mirroring `lift_support` (it reuses that namespace's AST helpers, one-slot streams, `serve-table`, header parts and `with-op-id`): `new-machine`, `project` with `source-index`, `parked-reader`, `parked-writer`, `parked-explicit`, `parked-linker`, `parked-installer`, `halted-machine`, `halted-with-cursors`, `parked-module-store-cursors`, `lift`, `attacher` (counting), `receiver` |\n42\t| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc` | 452 | Five bases lifted under a header; 50 mutations (task edits re-emitted, raw pack edits where the defect is the carrier's own); `render`/`read-file`/`fixture`; editing helpers the authority tests use (`base`, `pack-of`, `edit-task`, `edit-custody`, `waits`, `update-put`, `set-op-id`, `update-child`, `enc`) |\n43\t| `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` | 917 | 55 pins, 3 anchors with full bytes |\n44\t| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_test.cljc` | 203 | 17 deftests |\n45\t| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc` | 275 | 11 deftests |\n46\t| `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/ledger-v2.txt` | 22 | Replaces `ledger-v1.txt` (deleted with `git rm`, so the deletion is staged) |\n47\t\n48\tChanged:\n49\t\n50\t- `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` (411 lines changed per `git diff --stat`, now 2219 lines):\n51\t  - `pack`: rows sorted by id, used by `emit`, `emit-numbered` and `frame->rows`.\n52\t  - The custody section: `max-exact`, `write-slots`, `custody-baseline`, `pack-address`.\n53\t  - `lift-task :header`: `header-custody` and `protect!`, `:op-seq-exhausted`, and the grammar run before emitting.\n54\t  - `check-custody!` rewritten as the custody stage: grammar, then fork under `:exclusive`, then `accept-grant!` ported verbatim. It runs once after the three per-tree stages and before `attach-streams!`.\n55\t  - `with-custody` in restore: issue numbering, gate, custody state, and the fenced gate.\n56\t- `authority/{grant,completion,admission}.cljc`: the require switched to `yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint` under the same alias, so each call site is unchanged; docstrings updated.\n57\t- Fixture bodies only:\n58\t  - Named in the plan: `ledger_fixtures`, `ledger_fixture_test` (docstring), `grant_test`, `completion_test`, `inherited_test`, `front_test`. `crash_cut_test` needed no edit; it follows `lf/park`.\n59\t  - Also needed (deviation 1): `admission_test`, `input_test`, `reclaim_test`, `holder/evidence_test`, `holder/reader_test`, `holder/writer_test`.\n60\t- `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc`: one expected value (deviation 2).\n61\t\n62\t## What the code does (for review)\n63\t\n64\t- **Custody slot (root slot 16):**\n65\t  - nil for a fork of any kind (owner Q1).\n66\t  - `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq}` plus `:yin.k/origin` for blocked or parked exclusive roots.\n67\t  - `{:yin.k/origin}` alone for a halted exclusive root.\n68\t  - nil on every child.\n69\t- **`custody-baseline`** is the version-1 header and op-id grammar of `checkpoint.cljc`, keyed off the slot. On top of that it:\n70\t  - refuses any key outside the five custody keys (`:custody-key`), so lease, epoch and holder cannot enter a body;\n71\t  - refuses a slot that is not a map (`:malformed-custody`);\n72\t  - checks each task's kind and role, that each `:install` pending names an install row, and that every child slot is nil;\n73\t  - refuses any op-id in a fork tree (`:fork-op-id`, closing continuation §11 Q4 for 7a).\n74\t\n75\t  Paths are relative to the root task: `[:custody k]`, `[:frames i :op-id …]`, and `[:installs <name> …]` inside children. The intent digest is BLAKE3 of the canonical `[:yin.k/append target-identity payload-slot]`, where `[:row n]` becomes `[:row id]`.\n76\t- **The lift** runs the grammar over the abstract task before emitting, the same contract as the version-1 lift running its inspector over its own bytes. It does not decode its own output, which keeps continuation §7.1 step 9 true. A fork lift of a machine that carries op-ids therefore refuses `:fork-op-id`; before 7a the op-ids travelled verbatim.\n77\t- **The lower's custody stage**, in order: grammar; then a fork under `:exclusive` is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; a halted exclusive root needs no grant and is gated `:ended`; `:fenced` skips the grant and gates `:exporting`, with waits moved to `:yin.k/recovery-waits`; otherwise `accept-grant!` runs. The grant's `:checkpoint` must equal `rows/pack-address` of the wire, not the root id; a test pins that the root id is refused as `:not-holder`.\n78\t- **Restore:**\n79\t  - `:yin.k/custody` comes from the evidence, with `:yin.k/next-op-seq` equal to the slot's value exactly.\n80\t  - In an exclusive tree every task numbers its `:put` waits (`:yin.k/issue`, `:yin.k/issued`) and is gated `:running`, as `resume-task*` does.\n81\t- **The two identities:**\n82\t  - The pack address is the ledger's `:yin.k/id`, the content-store key, the grant's checkpoint and the report's result.\n83\t  - The root id is the baseline's `:yin.k/root`.\n84\t  - Tested in `the-pack-address-is-storage-and-the-root-id-is-semantic`: address = BLAKE3 of the bytes; root ≠ address; root = the root row's own hash; rows sorted; re-emitting a decoded pack gives the same bytes. Also tested in `a-pack-variant-of-one-root-shares-its-baseline`.\n85\t\n86\t## Mutation checks\n87\t\n88\tDriver: `target/7a-mut/{mut,run}.py`. Each mutant was applied to a backup-guarded copy, run against `rows-custody-test`, `rows-checkpoint-test`, `authority.grant-test` and `ledger-fixture-test`, then restored. Each restore was byte-compared with a clean copy; all were clean.\n89\t\n90\t| # | Mutant | Caught? | By |\n91\t|---|---|---|---|\n92\t| M1 | Custody header by kind swapped (`header-custody` branches) | yes | `the-lift-writes-the-custody-slot-by-kind`, every base (they no longer lift), 46 deftests |\n93\t| M2 | A grant check skipped (holder vs binding) | yes | `every-grant-check-refuses-before-any-attachment` (\"another holder\") |\n94\t| M3 | Protection, one direction dropped (an enrolled stream classed otherwise accepted) | **survived at first**; yes after the fix | The op-id presence check that follows masked it on the successor. I added the isolating case (a reader pack with no retained write) to `protection-is-checked-in-both-directions` |\n95\t| M4 | Counter restore off by one | yes | `the-counter-is-restored-exactly`, `an-exclusive-root-lowers-under-its-grant`, `install-children-lower-…` |\n96\t| M5 | Fork under `:exclusive` accepted | yes | `a-fork-is-refused-under-exclusive` |\n97\t| M6 | Custody stage after attach | yes | The zero-attach assertions in 4 deftests (30 assertions) |\n98\t| M7 | Unsorted `emit` | yes | `the-file-holds-exactly-the-declared-bytes` (anchors), `the-pack-address-…`, the ledger frames, 14 deftests |\n99\t| M8 | Inspector accepting a mutated row (decode's row-hash check off) | yes | `each-mutation-is-refused-as-it-names` (`row-tampered`) |\n100\t| M9 | Baseline root ignored | yes | 11 deftests, including `a-conflicting-variant-is-refused` (`variant-other-root`) and the ledger projection digest |\n101\t| M10 (extra) | The lift skips the grammar | yes | `the-lift-refuses-what-custody-forbids` |\n102\t\n103\t## Deviations\n104\t\n105\t1. **Six more test files rebased.** `authority/{admission,input,reclaim}_test` and `holder/{evidence,reader,writer}_test` offer fixture bodies through `grant/offer!` and break without the switch. Each change is a three-to-five-line `body` helper.\n106\t2. **One harness assertion changed.** `carrier-steps-run-in-order-over-the-tree` expected the phase-6 refusal `[:yin.k/unsupported :custody]` for slot `{:custody :exclusive}`. The plan replaces `check-custody!`, so it now expects `[:yin.k/undecodable :custody-key]`. Nothing else in the harness changed, and no root id moved.\n107\t3. **`:fenced` is minimal.** It skips the grant and protection, gates `:exporting` on every task and moves the waits to `:yin.k/recovery-waits`. `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids`, the exact id counter, the recovery header check and moving parked records into the record are left to 7b (plan §3.2 costs the fenced mode there).\n108\t4. **Refusal kinds and paths are mine:**\n109\t   - New kinds: `:custody-key`, `:malformed-custody`, `:policy` (the old inspector gave a policy mismatch no kind), `:kind`, `:role`, `:child-header`, `:fork-op-id`, `:incomplete-install`.\n110\t   - Paths: `[:custody …]`, `[:frames i …]`, `[:installs <name> …]`.\n111\t   - Carrier-level refusals keep `rows/decode`'s own: a missing tag is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch :version` on rows, not `:undecodable :body` as on version 1.\n112\t5. **No op-id-on-variant fixture.** It is structurally impossible on rows: the pending table gives `:next` no op-id slot. I added fixtures the old set lacked: `frame-root`, `row-tampered`, `root-role`, `custody-epoch`, `custody-lease`, `custody-not-map`, `fork-op-id`, `variant-other-root`.\n113\t6. **The grammar lives in `rows.cljc`** (`custody-baseline`). `rows.checkpoint` is a thin wrapper around it, because `lower-task` must run the grammar too and `rows.checkpoint` requires `rows` (a cycle otherwise). Behaviour is what plan §3.3 assigns to the inspector.\n114\t7. **Fixture shape.** `rows_checkpoint_fixtures` takes the `refusal` map a mutation must contain, instead of the old `expected` and `place` pair.\n115\t8. **Accessor alias kept.** The authority still calls the inspector through the alias `checkpoint`.\n116\t\n117\t## Design defects found (separate list)\n118\t\n119\t- **D1. 7a cannot land alone.** The authority switch (plan §2, §4 7a \"Change\") refuses version-1/2 bodies at `offer!`, `report!` and `accepted-baseline`. The holder, compose and Stage E paths still offer such bodies until 7b/7c, so 141 deftests in six suites fail (table above; proven by the counterfactual). The plan's 7a acceptance never mentions them.\n120\t  - Options for the orchestrator:\n121\t    1. Merge 7a only together with 7b and 7c.\n122\t    2. Move the three-line authority switch out of 7a into 7c.\n123\t    3. A temporary version dispatch in the authority. This contradicts \"no version dispatch\" and is not recommended.\n124\t  - `handoff_v2_authority_test` (an old-codec test the plan keeps until phase 8) also breaks permanently under any option. It needs deleting or re-basing, which the plan does not list.\n125\t- **D2. Plan §1.1 row-1 contradicts §3.3.** Row-1 wants a re-emitted id-counter variant with \"a new root id and new pack; equal baseline\". With `:yin.k/root` in the baseline, which this brief requires, such a variant's baseline differs, and the ledger refuses it as `:variant-conflict` (pinned by `grant_test` `variant-other-root`). Under sorted `emit`, the only equal-baseline variant is the same root in another row order. I used that for `variant-equal`, `completion_test` and `inherited_test`.\n126\t  - Stage E row-1 (7d) needs a ruling: either the variant is a conflict, or the root leaves the baseline.\n127\t  - Related: `rows/decode` accepts rows in any order, so \"one root, one pack\" holds for what `emit` makes, not for what a reader accepts. Should decode refuse unsorted packs? I left decode unchanged.\n128\t- **D3. Draft amendments to list (no doc edited):**\n129\t  - transport-tuples §5.5 table: fork is nil in every row (owner Q1), and the slot admits only the five custody keys.\n130\t  - continuation §2.5, §2.6, §7.1 step 8 and §7.2's stage table: custody is not phase-6 nil-only any more; the step-10 refusal `:yin.k/unsupported :custody` is replaced by the grammar's kinds and the grant refusals; the stage runs once for the tree after the three per-tree stages.\n131\t  - continuation §11 Q4: closed for the op-id rules (correlation agreement is still not implemented).\n132\t  - UCF 7.11.1: the inspector citation moves from `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint` to `yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint`.\n133\t- **D4. The lift serves streams before custody refusals.** v1 refused `:op-seq-exhausted` before lifting; rows refuses it after the walk has called `serve!`. Harmless to the lower's zero-attach law, but a holder that counts serves will see them (7b's export record).\n134\t\n135\t## What 7b needs from me\n136\t\n137\t- `rows/lift-task` with `{:header h :source-index …}` is `holder.export/prepare` and `encode`'s new path; `h` is the same header shape (`:yin.k/enrolled` stays local).\n138\t- `rows/pack-address wire` is the storage key to store and fence. The grant's `:checkpoint` must be that address.\n139\t- `rows/lower-task recv wire attach! {:grant g :protection p}` is the driver's `lower`; `{:exclusive true}` refuses a fork; `{:fenced true}` is the rehydration entry. Finishing fenced is 7b's: recovery-cell-ids, exact id counter, parked records into the record, the header check against `:yin.k/state`.\n140\t- `rows.checkpoint/inspect` answers the old inspector's keys plus `:yin.k/root`; `reopen-holder` and `rebuilt-cell` can read `:yin.k/kind`, `:yin.k/occurrence` and `:yin.k/next-op-seq` from it.\n141\t- `rows_support` builders for the driver, writer and reader tests, and `rows_checkpoint_fixtures`' editing helpers for packs.\n142\t- D1 decides whether 7b lands together with 7a.\n143\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"72b4f199-a30e-42b5-b648-90d6dc2ab934","uuid":"693fa4ba-3909-45a6-83e6-80cdd6b7a9ee","timestamp":"2026-10-13T13:58:12.863Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7b/collab/7a-report.md","content":"Created-Local: 2026-10-13 +07\n# Slice 7a report: custody on rows, the rows inspector, the authority's fixtures (opus, engineer)\n\nWorktree `srvm-phase7a`, from `23a8caef`. Nothing committed. JVM only, all runs in the foreground (one quick-tier run of every `yin.vm.ucf.*` namespace went past the 10-minute foreground limit, finished in the background and was read from its log).\n\n## Verdict\n\n7a is done as the plan's slice section and §3.1, §3.3 and §3.4 describe it, and its own acceptance holds: the two new suites, the ledger fixture, crash cuts, the eight authority tests and the holder tests that use fixtures all pass. `rows_test` and the safepoint harness pass with **no root id moved**.\n\n**7a cannot land alone (design defect D1 below).** The plan's switch of the authority to the rows inspector makes the authority refuse every version-1/2 body. 141 deftests in six old-codec D/E suites now fail. Those suites are re-based in 7b/7c, and I proved the switch is the only cause. I did not work around it, because a fix would change semantics; the decision belongs to the orchestrator.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Lint: `clojure -M:kondo` needed approval in this session, so I used `clj -M:kondo --lint <files>`. Final run over all 22 changed `.clj[c]` files: 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n- Quick tier, new and touched namespaces plus `rows_test`, the harness, `layout_test`, the old `checkpoint_test` and `lift_v1_test` (20 namespaces): **360 tests, 7188 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors** (`target/7a-final.log`).\n  - `rows_checkpoint_test`: 17 deftests.\n  - `rows_custody_test`: 11 deftests, 107 assertions before the M3 fix added two more.\n- Slow tier (`-i :slow`) on the same namespaces plus `rows_test` and the harness: 4 tests, 2724 assertions, 0 failures.\n- Every `yin.vm.ucf.*` namespace, quick tier (`-r 'yin\\.vm\\.ucf\\..*'`, `target/7a-ucf-quick.log`): 694 tests, 648 failures and 89 errors. They are all in six suites (see D1):\n\n  | Namespace | Failing deftests | Failing assertions |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | `holder.driver-test` | 92 | 294 |\n  | `compose-rows-test` | 35 | 335 |\n  | `compose-test` | 6 | 35 |\n  | `stage-e-rows-test` | 4 (the quick ones) | 35 |\n  | `fencing-rows-test` | 3 | 30 |\n  | `handoff-v2-authority-test` | 1 | 8 |\n\n  No other `yin.vm.ucf` namespace fails.\n- Counterfactual check: I pointed the three authority requires back at `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint` and ran `fencing-rows-test`, `handoff-v2-authority-test` and `compose-test`: 21 tests, 332 assertions, 0 failures. I then restored the files (byte-compare verified).\n- Node and Dart were not run, as instructed. The new code follows the portability rules: no `for`, no private var access, catches with `:cljd` first, start-from-`{}` assoc, no EDN strings. The `version-float` fixture relies on Node keeping a `Float64` carrier and on Dart's `integer?` rejecting a double, both checked in the source (`rows/decode` gates on `code/reg?`), but this is unverified on those hosts.\n\n## Files\n\nCreated:\n\n| File | Lines | What |\n|---|---|---|\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows/checkpoint.cljc` | 102 | The rows inspector: `inspect address bytes`, `inspect-pack wire`. Address check, canonical CBOR, `rows/decode`, a `:task` root, then `rows/custody-baseline`; adds `:yin.k/root` |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_support.cljc` | 238 | Semantic-register machine builders mirroring `lift_support` (it reuses that namespace's AST helpers, one-slot streams, `serve-table`, header parts and `with-op-id`): `new-machine`, `project` with `source-index`, `parked-reader`, `parked-writer`, `parked-explicit`, `parked-linker`, `parked-installer`, `halted-machine`, `halted-with-cursors`, `parked-module-store-cursors`, `lift`, `attacher` (counting), `receiver` |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc` | 452 | Five bases lifted under a header; 50 mutations (task edits re-emitted, raw pack edits where the defect is the carrier's own); `render`/`read-file`/`fixture`; editing helpers the authority tests use (`base`, `pack-of`, `edit-task`, `edit-custody`, `waits`, `update-put`, `set-op-id`, `update-child`, `enc`) |\n| `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` | 917 | 55 pins, 3 anchors with full bytes |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_test.cljc` | 203 | 17 deftests |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc` | 275 | 11 deftests |\n| `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/ledger-v2.txt` | 22 | Replaces `ledger-v1.txt` (deleted with `git rm`, so the deletion is staged) |\n\nChanged:\n\n- `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` (411 lines changed per `git diff --stat`, now 2219 lines):\n  - `pack`: rows sorted by id, used by `emit`, `emit-numbered` and `frame->rows`.\n  - The custody section: `max-exact`, `write-slots`, `custody-baseline`, `pack-address`.\n  - `lift-task :header`: `header-custody` and `protect!`, `:op-seq-exhausted`, and the grammar run before emitting.\n  - `check-custody!` rewritten as the custody stage: grammar, then fork under `:exclusive`, then `accept-grant!` ported verbatim. It runs once after the three per-tree stages and before `attach-streams!`.\n  - `with-custody` in restore: issue numbering, gate, custody state, and the fenced gate.\n- `authority/{grant,completion,admission}.cljc`: the require switched to `yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint` under the same alias, so each call site is unchanged; docstrings updated.\n- Fixture bodies only:\n  - Named in the plan: `ledger_fixtures`, `ledger_fixture_test` (docstring), `grant_test`, `completion_test`, `inherited_test`, `front_test`. `crash_cut_test` needed no edit; it follows `lf/park`.\n  - Also needed (deviation 1): `admission_test`, `input_test`, `reclaim_test`, `holder/evidence_test`, `holder/reader_test`, `holder/writer_test`.\n- `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc`: one expected value (deviation 2).\n\n## What the code does (for review)\n\n- **Custody slot (root slot 16):**\n  - nil for a fork of any kind (owner Q1).\n  - `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq}` plus `:yin.k/origin` for blocked or parked exclusive roots.\n  - `{:yin.k/origin}` alone for a halted exclusive root.\n  - nil on every child.\n- **`custody-baseline`** is the version-1 header and op-id grammar of `checkpoint.cljc`, keyed off the slot. On top of that it:\n  - refuses any key outside the five custody keys (`:custody-key`), so lease, epoch and holder cannot enter a body;\n  - refuses a slot that is not a map (`:malformed-custody`);\n  - checks each task's kind and role, that each `:install` pending names an install row, and that every child slot is nil;\n  - refuses any op-id in a fork tree (`:fork-op-id`, closing continuation §11 Q4 for 7a).\n\n  Paths are relative to the root task: `[:custody k]`, `[:frames i :op-id …]`, and `[:installs <name> …]` inside children. The intent digest is BLAKE3 of the canonical `[:yin.k/append target-identity payload-slot]`, where `[:row n]` becomes `[:row id]`.\n- **The lift** runs the grammar over the abstract task before emitting, the same contract as the version-1 lift running its inspector over its own bytes. It does not decode its own output, which keeps continuation §7.1 step 9 true. A fork lift of a machine that carries op-ids therefore refuses `:fork-op-id`; before 7a the op-ids travelled verbatim.\n- **The lower's custody stage**, in order: grammar; then a fork under `:exclusive` is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; a halted exclusive root needs no grant and is gated `:ended`; `:fenced` skips the grant and gates `:exporting`, with waits moved to `:yin.k/recovery-waits`; otherwise `accept-grant!` runs. The grant's `:checkpoint` must equal `rows/pack-address` of the wire, not the root id; a test pins that the root id is refused as `:not-holder`.\n- **Restore:**\n  - `:yin.k/custody` comes from the evidence, with `:yin.k/next-op-seq` equal to the slot's value exactly.\n  - In an exclusive tree every task numbers its `:put` waits (`:yin.k/issue`, `:yin.k/issued`) and is gated `:running`, as `resume-task*` does.\n- **The two identities:**\n  - The pack address is the ledger's `:yin.k/id`, the content-store key, the grant's checkpoint and the report's result.\n  - The root id is the baseline's `:yin.k/root`.\n  - Tested in `the-pack-address-is-storage-and-the-root-id-is-semantic`: address = BLAKE3 of the bytes; root ≠ address; root = the root row's own hash; rows sorted; re-emitting a decoded pack gives the same bytes. Also tested in `a-pack-variant-of-one-root-shares-its-baseline`.\n\n## Mutation checks\n\nDriver: `target/7a-mut/{mut,run}.py`. Each mutant was applied to a backup-guarded copy, run against `rows-custody-test`, `rows-checkpoint-test`, `authority.grant-test` and `ledger-fixture-test`, then restored. Each restore was byte-compared with a clean copy; all were clean.\n\n| # | Mutant | Caught? | By |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| M1 | Custody header by kind swapped (`header-custody` branches) | yes | `the-lift-writes-the-custody-slot-by-kind`, every base (they no longer lift), 46 deftests |\n| M2 | A grant check skipped (holder vs binding) | yes | `every-grant-check-refuses-before-any-attachment` (\"another holder\") |\n| M3 | Protection, one direction dropped (an enrolled stream classed otherwise accepted) | **survived at first**; yes after the fix | The op-id presence check that follows masked it on the successor. I added the isolating case (a reader pack with no retained write) to `protection-is-checked-in-both-directions` |\n| M4 | Counter restore off by one | yes | `the-counter-is-restored-exactly`, `an-exclusive-root-lowers-under-its-grant`, `install-children-lower-…` |\n| M5 | Fork under `:exclusive` accepted | yes | `a-fork-is-refused-under-exclusive` |\n| M6 | Custody stage after attach | yes | The zero-attach assertions in 4 deftests (30 assertions) |\n| M7 | Unsorted `emit` | yes | `the-file-holds-exactly-the-declared-bytes` (anchors), `the-pack-address-…`, the ledger frames, 14 deftests |\n| M8 | Inspector accepting a mutated row (decode's row-hash check off) | yes | `each-mutation-is-refused-as-it-names` (`row-tampered`) |\n| M9 | Baseline root ignored | yes | 11 deftests, including `a-conflicting-variant-is-refused` (`variant-other-root`) and the ledger projection digest |\n| M10 (extra) | The lift skips the grammar | yes | `the-lift-refuses-what-custody-forbids` |\n\n## Deviations\n\n1. **Six more test files rebased.** `authority/{admission,input,reclaim}_test` and `holder/{evidence,reader,writer}_test` offer fixture bodies through `grant/offer!` and break without the switch. Each change is a three-to-five-line `body` helper.\n2. **One harness assertion changed.** `carrier-steps-run-in-order-over-the-tree` expected the phase-6 refusal `[:yin.k/unsupported :custody]` for slot `{:custody :exclusive}`. The plan replaces `check-custody!`, so it now expects `[:yin.k/undecodable :custody-key]`. Nothing else in the harness changed, and no root id moved.\n3. **`:fenced` is minimal.** It skips the grant and protection, gates `:exporting` on every task and moves the waits to `:yin.k/recovery-waits`. `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids`, the exact id counter, the recovery header check and moving parked records into the record are left to 7b (plan §3.2 costs the fenced mode there).\n4. **Refusal kinds and paths are mine:**\n   - New kinds: `:custody-key`, `:malformed-custody`, `:policy` (the old inspector gave a policy mismatch no kind), `:kind`, `:role`, `:child-header`, `:fork-op-id`, `:incomplete-install`.\n   - Paths: `[:custody …]`, `[:frames i …]`, `[:installs <name> …]`.\n   - Carrier-level refusals keep `rows/decode`'s own: a missing tag is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch :version` on rows, not `:undecodable :body` as on version 1.\n5. **No op-id-on-variant fixture.** It is structurally impossible on rows: the pending table gives `:next` no op-id slot. I added fixtures the old set lacked: `frame-root`, `row-tampered`, `root-role`, `custody-epoch`, `custody-lease`, `custody-not-map`, `fork-op-id`, `variant-other-root`.\n6. **The grammar lives in `rows.cljc`** (`custody-baseline`). `rows.checkpoint` is a thin wrapper around it, because `lower-task` must run the grammar too and `rows.checkpoint` requires `rows` (a cycle otherwise). Behaviour is what plan §3.3 assigns to the inspector.\n7. **Fixture shape.** `rows_checkpoint_fixtures` takes the `refusal` map a mutation must contain, instead of the old `expected` and `place` pair.\n8. **Accessor alias kept.** The authority still calls the inspector through the alias `checkpoint`.\n\n## Design defects found (separate list)\n\n- **D1. 7a cannot land alone.** The authority switch (plan §2, §4 7a \"Change\") refuses version-1/2 bodies at `offer!`, `report!` and `accepted-baseline`. The holder, compose and Stage E paths still offer such bodies until 7b/7c, so 141 deftests in six suites fail (table above; proven by the counterfactual). The plan's 7a acceptance never mentions them.\n  - Options for the orchestrator:\n    1. Merge 7a only together with 7b and 7c.\n    2. Move the three-line authority switch out of 7a into 7c.\n    3. A temporary version dispatch in the authority. This contradicts \"no version dispatch\" and is not recommended.\n  - `handoff_v2_authority_test` (an old-codec test the plan keeps until phase 8) also breaks permanently under any option. It needs deleting or re-basing, which the plan does not list.\n- **D2. Plan §1.1 row-1 contradicts §3.3.** Row-1 wants a re-emitted id-counter variant with \"a new root id and new pack; equal baseline\". With `:yin.k/root` in the baseline, which this brief requires, such a variant's baseline differs, and the ledger refuses it as `:variant-conflict` (pinned by `grant_test` `variant-other-root`). Under sorted `emit`, the only equal-baseline variant is the same root in another row order. I used that for `variant-equal`, `completion_test` and `inherited_test`.\n  - Stage E row-1 (7d) needs a ruling: either the variant is a conflict, or the root leaves the baseline.\n  - Related: `rows/decode` accepts rows in any order, so \"one root, one pack\" holds for what `emit` makes, not for what a reader accepts. Should decode refuse unsorted packs? I left decode unchanged.\n- **D3. Draft amendments to list (no doc edited):**\n  - transport-tuples §5.5 table: fork is nil in every row (owner Q1), and the slot admits only the five custody keys.\n  - continuation §2.5, §2.6, §7.1 step 8 and §7.2's stage table: custody is not phase-6 nil-only any more; the step-10 refusal `:yin.k/unsupported :custody` is replaced by the grammar's kinds and the grant refusals; the stage runs once for the tree after the three per-tree stages.\n  - continuation §11 Q4: closed for the op-id rules (correlation agreement is still not implemented).\n  - UCF 7.11.1: the inspector citation moves from `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint` to `yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint`.\n- **D4. The lift serves streams before custody refusals.** v1 refused `:op-seq-exhausted` before lifting; rows refuses it after the walk has called `serve!`. Harmless to the lower's zero-attach law, but a holder that counts serves will see them (7b's export record).\n\n## What 7b needs from me\n\n- `rows/lift-task` with `{:header h :source-index …}` is `holder.export/prepare` and `encode`'s new path; `h` is the same header shape (`:yin.k/enrolled` stays local).\n- `rows/pack-address wire` is the storage key to store and fence. The grant's `:checkpoint` must be that address.\n- `rows/lower-task recv wire attach! {:grant g :protection p}` is the driver's `lower`; `{:exclusive true}` refuses a fork; `{:fenced true}` is the rehydration entry. Finishing fenced is 7b's: recovery-cell-ids, exact id counter, parked records into the record, the header check against `:yin.k/state`.\n- `rows.checkpoint/inspect` answers the old inspector's keys plus `:yin.k/root`; `reopen-holder` and `rebuilt-cell` can read `:yin.k/kind`, `:yin.k/occurrence` and `:yin.k/next-op-seq` from it.\n- `rows_support` builders for the driver, writer and reader tests, and `rows_checkpoint_fixtures`' editing helpers for packs.\n- D1 decides whether 7b lands together with 7a.\n","numLines":143,"startLine":1,"totalLines":143}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01BJwzdj2FJCGN8U6J7tgixE","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
